3 (a). Two main reasons to use eventual consistency here:
First is, for certain types of metadata, such as view counts and ratings, occasional inconsistencies may be acceptable to users. Users might be more concerned with the overall trends rather than the precise real-time values.
Second is that, we just have to respond within 20 ms, we should definitely go with eventual consistency as it allows for quicker response times, as it doesn't require waiting for all nodes to be immediately synchronized. If we will use strong consistency, then it won't allow read till all the nodes get synchronized.
Then it will also help us scale the solution and make it HIGHLY AVAILABLE.
Prioritizing eventual consistency increases the availability of the system. Even if some nodes are temporarily unreachable, the system can continue functioning and converge to a consistent state over time.

3 (b). Can you please help me with the scalability requirement of the system?
If we have a high scalability requirement, then strong consistency may introduce contention on write operations, as all nodes need to agree on the ORDER OF CLICKS in real-time. Eventual consistency allows for more relaxed coordination, reducing contention and enhancing write performance.
And as this is for analytical purpose, so eventual consistency should be fine for this case as it don't need to be the 100% latest data I guess.

3 (c). Financial transactions, such as deposits and payments, require strong consistency to ensure that each transaction is executed atomically and consistently. All nodes in the system should agree on the state of an account after a transaction.
Strong consistency guarantees that any read operation after a write will return the most recent write. This is crucial for maintaining accurate and up-to-date account balances, preventing discrepancies that could arise from eventual consistency.

