5. On the TCP level (Web Socket) the tuple (source ip, source port, destination ip, destination port) must be unique for each simultaneous connection. So in practice the problem becomes one of resources such as CPU power, memory, etc. For example, let's say a single machine wants to support 64,000 simultaneous connections. If the server uses 1MB of RAM per connection, it would need 64GB of RAM. If each client needs to read a file, the disk or storage array access load becomes much larger than those devices can handle. If a server needs to fork one process per connection then the OS will spend the majority of its time context switching or starving processes for CPU time.
